WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
Look at these two rendering outputs.

2
00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.440
On the left, a fluid stream of UI elements.

3
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.880
On the right, the exact same interaction

4
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.640
stutters and pauses.

5
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.000
If you are an iOS engineer transitioning to React Native,

6
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.640
you've likely encountered this exact frame-rate drop

7
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.880
when building complex lists or nested screens.

8
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.880
React's render engine is fast.

9
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.280
The performance drop on the right

10
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.720
is caused by the architecture itself.

11
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.680
When components are deeply nested,

12
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.920
an update at the top can trigger a cascade

13
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.240
of unnecessary re-renders all the way down the tree.

14
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.840
To fix this, you don't need to learn

15
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.960
a completely foreign way of thinking.

16
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.440
The computer science principles

17
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.200
to stabilize a React rendering loop

18
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.960
are identical to the memory management

19
00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.400
and view and validation logic you already use in Swift.

20
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.080
We will bypass the generic React tutorials.

21
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.600
Instead, we map the optimization patterns you know

22
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.840
from Swift UI directly to their React equivalents.

23
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.520
Achieving native level performance in React Native

24
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.640
requires translating your established iOS intuition

25
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.560
into the React lifecycle.

26
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.440
This translation matrix provides a one-to-one mapping

27
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.280
between iOS paradigms and React hooks.

28
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.600
We'll fill this board, mapping Swift UI in validation,

29
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.680
computed properties, and state enums

30
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.960
directly to their React counterparts.

31
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.280
The strict rule, apply these surgically

32
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.920
based on profiling data.

33
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.360
Internalizing this map solves

34
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.960
the most common beginner mistake in React Native.

35
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.680
You stop fighting the render engine

36
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.520
and start cooperating with it.

37
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.720
In Swift, when a Swift UI view conforms

38
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.160
to the equatable protocol, you dictate recalculations.

39
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.320
The compiler checks the specific values the view depends on.

40
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.160
If unchanged, Swift UI skips re-evaluating the body entirely.

41
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.160
React's exact equivalent is React.memo.

42
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.680
Like Swift UI, it uses shallow comparison,

43
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.040
checking if primitive values or memory references

44
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.560
match the previous render.

45
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.480
If props remain identical, the child skips rendering.

46
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.920
For complex structures, React.nemo

47
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.360
accepts a custom comparison function

48
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.000
like manual double equals in Swift.

49
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.120
Rapping a component in React.memo

50
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.320
is the necessary first step for caching your UI,

51
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.880
but it immediately exposes how React handles memory references

52
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.560
differently than Swift.

53
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.480
Watch how React executes this functional component.

54
00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.520
Everything inside the body is recreated from scratch,

55
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.360
receiving a new memory address on every render.

56
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.240
Passing this newly minted function

57
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.800
to a React.memo child instantly breaks the cache.

58
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.520
The child component receives a function

59
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.480
with a brand new memory reference.

60
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.360
It fails the shallow equality check

61
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.240
and triggers a full unnecessary re-render.

62
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.680
In iOS, you prevent this kind of redundant work

63
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.640
but using computed properties, isolating derivations

64
00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.240
so they only recalculate when their specific base values change.

65
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.320
React handles this with use memo.

66
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.520
A dependency array tells React to hold the previous value,

67
00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.160
recalculating only when dependencies change.

68
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.560
For functions, the equivalent to Swift's stored closures

69
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.320
is used callback.

70
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.360
It guarantees reference stability,

71
00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.320
ensuring the child components sees the exact same memory address.

72
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.400
This also applies to objects.

73
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.120
An empty object literal creates a new reference every render.

74
00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.640
If you pass an object as a prop or inject it

75
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.680
into another hooks dependency list,

76
00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.960
it must be wrapped in use memo.

77
00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.200
React.memo, use memo, and use callback,

78
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.560
operate as a coordinated trio.

79
00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.440
Failing to stabilize even one prop reference

80
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.760
invalidates the entire caching strategy

81
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:24.400
for that UI branch.

82
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.120
Let's trace a state update using React's context API.

83
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.120
The central provider node distributes a state object.

84
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:33.960
When updated, it generates a new object literal,

85
00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:37.160
forcing every single consumer in the tree to re-render,

86
00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.880
even if their specific value hasn't changed.

87
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.680
The immediate fix is wrapping the context payload in use memo.

88
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.640
Now, the reference remains stable,

89
00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.200
and consumers only re-render

90
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.880
when the underlying data actually updates.

91
00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.400
But wrapping everything in use memo is a patch.

92
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.920
The superior architectural solution, matching SwiftUI,

93
00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:00.280
is components splitting to isolate state dependencies.

94
00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:01.680
The rule is simple.

95
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.600
Move state to the closest component that needs it.

96
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.240
By decentralizing state away from the root,

97
00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.880
React naturally invalidates and re-renders

98
00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:12.160
smaller, specific subviews.

99
00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.800
When you have multiple state properties

100
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.360
that must change together, Swift developers

101
00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.600
model these coordinated transitions using

102
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.320
unums or immutable structs with dedicated transition methods.

103
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.800
React's direct equivalent for complex isolated state logic

104
00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.280
is use reducer.

105
00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.680
Beyond organizing complex transitions,

106
00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.200
use reducer has a hidden performance feature.

107
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.920
Its dispatch function inherently

108
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.800
maintains a stable memory reference between renders.

109
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.840
You can pass it deep into your component tree

110
00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.760
without ever needing use callback.

111
00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.520
Relying on architectural boundaries and tools

112
00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.120
like use reducer solves performance bottlenecks by design.

113
00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.320
Long before you have to patch them with memoization hooks,

114
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.320
this horizontal timeline maps how iOS engineers

115
00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.680
prioritize UI responsiveness.

116
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.520
Urgent main thread work is strictly

117
00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.000
separated from heavy processing by pushing

118
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.800
the heavy loads to a background dispatch queue.

119
00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.160
React 18 introduced a concurrent equivalent, start transition.

120
00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.280
By marking a specific state update as non-urgent,

121
00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:13.280
the render engine can literally pause a heavy list update

122
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.880
to handle immediate high priority input,

123
00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.600
like typing in a search bar.

124
00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.840
By utilizing the is pending flag,

125
00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.840
you can display a loading state and achieve

126
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.480
zero latency UI interactions while complex state changes

127
00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.480
continue rendering in the background.

128
00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.000
Memoization hooks exist to avoid unnecessary renders.

129
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.040
Start transition dictates the priority

130
00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:35.160
of the renders that are necessary, ensuring

131
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.160
smooth interactions without blocking the main thread.

132
00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.800
You do not apply the lazy keyword to every single property

133
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.400
in a Swift application.

134
00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.440
That is premature optimization

135
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.480
and the exact same logic applies to React.

136
00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.520
Look at the execution time of a memoized component.

137
00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.800
React.memo, use memo, and use callback are not free.

138
00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.800
They execute an active computational cost.

139
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.360
Under the hood, React is actively storing previous values,

140
00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:04.600
iterating through arrays of dependencies

141
00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:08.040
and managing cache memory for every memoized component.

142
00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.440
If your component is simple and only renders primitive elements,

143
00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.960
performing that cache comparison takes more time

144
00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:17.120
than just letting React re-render the component natively.

145
00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.880
There are strict mathematical anti-patterns here.

146
00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.600
Do not memoize if a component almost always receives

147
00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:25.360
different props anyway and do not use use memo

148
00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.360
for trivial calculations like basic arithmetic.

149
00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.320
Blanket memoization doesn't speed up an application.

150
00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.240
It transforms a lightweight UI into a heavy cache bloated system

151
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.680
actively fighting its own render engine.

152
00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.720
The golden rule of rendering across all platforms

153
00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.720
is measure first, optimize later.

154
00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.040
This React dev tools flame chart is the exact equivalent

155
00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.480
to the time profiler.

156
00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.360
It maps the component hierarchy during a commit.

157
00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.960
Look at the why did this render metadata?

158
00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:57.040
If it states parent re-rendered with identical props

159
00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.400
that mathematically justifies applying React.memo.

160
00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.720
To monitor this at scale, you can implement

161
00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:04.560
the why did you render library.

162
00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.960
It automatically prints unnecessary re-renders to the console,

163
00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.680
specifically targeting components that

164
00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:11.800
receive the exact same values housed

165
00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:13.560
in different memory references.

166
00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.880
Here is the completed translation matrix.

167
00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.440
True React native mastery for an iOS engineer

168
00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:21.520
does not require memorizing arbitrary lifecycle hooks.

169
00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:24.440
It requires projecting your existing architectural discipline

170
00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.920
onto a new profiling tool and only executing optimizations

171
00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.600
when the data explicitly demands it.

